// Two imported modules each declare a generic value record named `Key<T>` with
// DIFFERENT payload types (`keyleft.Key<T>.v: i64` vs `keyright.Key<T>.v:
// string`). Both are instantiated at `i32` in one program, so each module's
// declaration must reach codegen under its own module-qualified layout symbol
// (`hew$keyleft$Key$$i32` and `hew$keyright$Key$$i32`), never one shared
// `Key$$i32`.
//
// Layout identity is declaration identity: `mangle_layout_key` carries the
// owner module through `mangle_dotted_name`, and the type-argument spine keeps
// its canonical paths. The run.sh block for this fixture pins both qualified
// symbols in the emitted IR and asserts the bare `Key$$i32` never appears.
//
// Contrast the SINGLE-module case (`imported_generic_valueclass.hew`), which
// covers an imported generic value record with no same-named twin.
//
// Harness verb: hew run
// Expected: each field read returns its own module's payload.
import hew.keyleft;

import hew.keyright;

fn main() {
    let a: keyleft.Key<i32> = keyleft.mk(12345);
    let b: keyright.Key<i32> = keyright.mk("hello");
    println(a.v);
    println(b.v);
}
